Generate the interrupt code in a fixed order.

The macro collects the interrupt numbers in a HashMap and emits the
match arms and the handler declarations in the map's iteration order,
which changes on every compile. Each build of a crate that uses the
macro then has a different crate hash, so a build on one host does
not link against a crate that another host built, and the build stops
with E0463. A BTreeMap emits the items in number order on every
compile.

Applies to riscv-macros 0.3.0 from crates.io; the pin and this patch
are consumed by nix/patched-crates.nix.

